format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

objective u2-decay-chain
statement: Know that a daughter can be unstable and decay again, until a stable nucleus is reached (a decay chain).
misconception: Every decay makes a stable atom.

item u2-l4-choice-finished
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-3

prompt:
    A sample has a nucleus whose daughter is also unstable. After the first decay, is the sample finished changing?

options:
    {"id":"no","label":"No: the daughter can decay too"}
    {"id":"yes-stable","label":"Yes: a decay always ends in a stable nucleus","note":"The daughter is unstable, so it can decay again."}
    {"id":"yes-gone","label":"Yes: the atom has disappeared","note":"Nothing vanishes. The nucleus changed into another nucleus."}

answer: no

explanation:
    No. The daughter is unstable, so the sample can keep changing along the chain.

item u2-l4-choice-next
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-3

prompt:
    A chain goes Parent, then Daughter. What comes next if the daughter is unstable?

options:
    {"id":"next","label":"The next generation, another nucleus"}
    {"id":"stable","label":"Always a stable nucleus","note":"The next nucleus can be unstable too. The chain only ends at a stable one."}
    {"id":"parent","label":"The parent again","note":"A decay does not run backwards."}

answer: next

explanation:
    An unstable daughter decays again into the next generation. The chain goes on until a stable nucleus is reached.

item u2-l4-order-chain
type: order
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-3

prompt:
    Put the chain in order, from the start to the end.

items:
    {"id":"parent","label":"Parent"}
    {"id":"daughter","label":"Daughter"}
    {"id":"next","label":"Next generation"}
    {"id":"stable","label":"Stable"}

explanation:
    A chain starts with the parent, goes through the daughter and the next generation, and ends at a stable nucleus.

item u2-l4-predict-daughter
type: predict
surfaces: lesson

prompt:
    A nucleus decays into a daughter. Is the daughter always stable?

input: choice

options:
    {"id":"yes","label":"Yes, every time"}
    {"id":"no","label":"No, sometimes it can be unstable too"}

answer: no

explanation:
    Your guess is not scored. The answer comes later in the lesson.

item u2-l4-tom-stable
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-3

statement:
    Every radioactive decay immediately creates a stable atom.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. The daughter can be unstable too, and then it decays again. A chain only ends at a stable nucleus.

item u2-x-chain-end
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-3

prompt:
    What ends a decay chain?

options:
    {"id":"stable","label":"A stable nucleus"}
    {"id":"first","label":"The first decay","note":"A daughter can be unstable and decay again."}
    {"id":"radiation","label":"The radiation running out","note":"The chain ends only when the nucleus is stable."}

answer: stable

explanation:
    A decay chain ends when a stable nucleus is reached.

item u2-x-fill-chain
type: fill
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-3

prompt:
    A sequence of decays in which one nucleus changes into another is called a decay ___.

mode: bank

bank:
    {"id":"chain","label":"chain"}
    {"id":"stop","label":"stop"}
    {"id":"clock","label":"clock"}

answer: chain

explanation:
    It is called a decay chain.
